from pathlib import Path
import json
D=Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent/'research/data/composition'
RESULTS=json.loads((D/'results.json').read_text())
CHECKS=json.loads((D/'verification.json').read_text())
TITLE='Effect Aware Composition of Learned Costs for Event Loop Blocking Prediction'
CONFERENCE='The 10th International Conference on Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence (CSAI 2026)'
KEYWORDS=['NEURO SYMBOLIC MODELS','EXECUTION GRAPHS','COST PREDICTION','COMPOSITIONAL GENERALISATION','EVENT LOOPS']
ABSTRACT='''Predicting a request's total work is insufficient to predict how long it can block an event loop. The same operations can form one uninterrupted region or several regions separated by suspension, while an awaited cache hit may not suspend at all. We model a request as an effect-annotated execution path, learn individual operation costs and compose them using sums within uninterrupted regions and a maximum across regions. A controlled benchmark contains 640 program variants (584 distinct paths) from 160 operation families, 6,400 executions across three loop and transport conditions, and 960 separate HTTP policy replays. Training compositions contain two or three blocking operations; longer tests contain four, six or eight. On the longer primary test, neural cost composition achieves 6.7% median absolute percentage error, compared with 16.9% for an order-aware recurrent model and 34.4% for an unordered tree ensemble. The recurrent model is more accurate on short tests, and parametric cost composition remains competitive. In HTTP replay, offloading 37.5% of programs reduces the 90th percentile of per-program maximum probe response times from 50.3 to 16.1 ms. The results identify suspension semantics as an explicit, testable source of compositional generalisation under controlled single-host conditions.'''

BLOCKS=[
("h1","1 Introduction"),
("p","A request's elapsed time and its event-loop blocking have different scheduling meanings. Waiting can increase completion time while allowing other tasks to run; uninterrupted computation delays them. A predictor using only a request address or an unordered operation summary can lose this distinction."),
("p","Event-handler blocking is an established systems problem. Davis et al. [1] analyse how monopolising an event handler can deny service to other clients. Python's coroutine semantics add a relevant modelling detail: awaiting a coroutine that returns immediately need not return control to the event loop [2]. A cache hit and a genuinely suspending operation may therefore have similar source-level syntax but different scheduling effects. The keyword await alone is not a sufficient boundary between blocking regions."),
("p","Moving an asynchronous wait from after two computations to between them can separate their blocking contributions without changing the operations or substantially changing total work. We test whether explicitly representing this execution relation improves prediction on compositions longer than those used to train a request-level model."),
("p","We study this question using an effect-annotated execution graph restricted to directed paths. Learned node costs describe the duration of individual operations. A fixed structural computation sums costs inside each uninterrupted region and takes the largest region. The experiment separates the value of operation learning, the value of order and the value of correct suspension annotations. An order-aware recurrent model receives the same operation and effect information, providing a stronger comparison than an unordered predictor alone."),
("p","Matched compositions expose information lost by unordered features; a conditional error bound describes the structural rule; and experiments test length transfer and HTTP interference. The neural regressor supplies operation costs. The practical goal is to identify which execution relations must remain explicit when predicting whether work should run on the event-loop thread."),
("h2","1.1 Relation to structured performance models"),
("p","Structured performance prediction spans different execution scales. Sage [3] diagnoses performance dependencies between microservices, whereas Ithemal [4] predicts assembly basic-block throughput with an ordered recurrent model. Our unit is one request's operation sequence, and the target is local event-loop interference rather than distributed root-cause diagnosis or hardware throughput."),
("p","Neural algorithmic reasoning [5] and the CLRS benchmark [6] examine learned algorithmic behaviour. More recently, open-book reasoning [7] uses training instances during inference to support CLRS tasks. Our sum-and-maximum aggregation remains fixed: learning estimates operation durations, while length transfer tests the value of preserving execution relations rather than learning a general graph algorithm."),
("p","Multivariate automatic amortised resource analysis (AARA) derives sound static bounds [8]. Hybrid AARA [9] integrates static constraints with Bayesian inference from observed costs. Chu et al. [10] extend AARA to exceptions and effect handlers, making non-local control transfer explicit. These analyses derive resource bounds from language semantics; our rule composes measured predictions under supplied suspension annotations, with a conditional error bound rather than a static worst-case guarantee."),
('p',"Pavlov [11] models completion times of competing distributed processes under limited software resources. This scheduling perspective motivates a separate boundary: our sequential-path estimate describes local uninterrupted work, while concurrent requests and shared-resource contention require a scheduler-level model."),
("h1","2 Materials and methods"),
("h2","2.1 Execution domain and suspension effects"),
('p',"A program is a finite directed path whose nodes are typed operations with size parameters. Computation nodes invoke PBKDF2 hashing, list generation and sorting, SQLite reading and aggregation, or image generation, resizing and JPEG encoding. Await nodes are annotated with whether the configured operation actually suspends. A true wait suspends for a nominal 3 ms; a cache-hit coroutine returns a small cached value immediately. Each node appears once in the path, and edges specify execution order. This representation includes heterogeneous operation types, numerical parameters and scheduling effects."),
("p","The effect annotation is part of the supplied domain description. In this benchmark it is known because the program configuration chooses a wait or a cache hit. We do not infer it from arbitrary Python source, predict an unknown future cache state or equate every asynchronous function with suspension. This makes the experiment an evaluation of the value of correct effects. Applications with uncertain effects would need a separate analysis or a conservative policy before using the model operationally."),
('figure',('effects.png','Fig. 1 The same computation nodes form different uninterrupted regions when suspension is moved or replaced by an immediate cache hit.')),
("p","Removing genuinely suspending nodes partitions computation into maximal uninterrupted regions; immediate coroutine returns remain within their surrounding region. Fig. 1 illustrates this transformation. A correctly annotated ordered list is equivalent for these paths, allowing the experiment to isolate execution relations from the choice of data structure."),
("h2","2.2 Cost composition and conditional error bound"),
("p","Let @G@ be an annotated path and let its uninterrupted regions form the set @R@(@G@). For computation node @i@, let @p_i@ be a positive predicted duration and @c_i@ its true duration in the specified environment. The predicted longest region is"),
('equation',('@B@(@G@) = max_R ∑_i @p_i@','1')),
('p',"In (1), the maximum ranges over regions in @R@(@G@), and the sum ranges over computation nodes in the selected region. The true longest region @b@(@G@) uses @c_i@ instead. Durations of suspending waits are excluded because another task can run during them. Small interpreter and I/O bookkeeping costs remain an approximation error. The algorithm makes one pass through the path, maintaining a current region sum and a maximum; its running time and auxiliary space are linear and constant, respectively, in the number of nodes."),
("p","For a fixed correct partition, the maximum operator and the triangle inequality give the conditional bound"),
('equation',('|@B@(@G@) − @b@(@G@)| ≤ max_R ∑_i |@p_i@ − @c_i@|','2')),
('p',"To verify (2), compare the predicted and true sum in every region. Their difference is no greater than the sum of the node errors there. The difference of the largest sums cannot exceed the largest such discrepancy. In particular, if every node estimate has relative error at most a common value, the composed longest-region estimate has at most that relative error. This is a property of the supplied aggregation rule, conditional on the node estimates and correct effects. It is not a bound on future HTTP response time or on an unobserved machine's execution cost."),
("p","There is also a simple limit for an unordered representation. Two matched programs with the same multiset and different placement of true waits have identical unordered features. A deterministic predictor must output the same value @q@ for both. If their positive target values are @a@ and @b@, their mean absolute error is at least |@a@ − @b@|/2. The worst relative error within the pair obeys"),
('equation',('@E@(@q@) ≥ |@a@ − @b@| / (@a@ + @b@)','3')),
("p","Here @E@(@q@) is the larger of |@q@ − @a@|/@a@ and |@q@ − @b@|/@b@. The bound follows by requiring a common prediction to lie within both relative-error intervals; they first intersect when the relative tolerance reaches the right-hand side. This elementary observation concerns indistinguishable inputs. It neither limits an order-aware model nor implies that the pooled median error must exceed the pairwise bound."),
("h2","2.3 Operations and measurement targets"),
("p","The experiment reuses the operation implementations of an existing Python request-processing testbed but collects a new dataset. PBKDF2 uses SHA-256 and 20,000–160,000 iterations. Sorting generates and sorts 10,000–100,000 pseudorandom values. Image processing uses side lengths of 128–640 pixels. SQLite reads 1,000–20,000 rows from a fixed 20,000-row database and aggregates the returned records. These operations perform real computation and data access; their compositions are deliberately generated experimental programs, not production traces."),
("p","Calibration measures each operation type at 12 logarithmically spaced size settings, with five repetitions in shuffled order: 48 settings and 240 timing observations. A model is fitted to the median duration at each setting. Calibration covers the size range used in compositions. Consequently, the principal transfer question is new composition and greater length; the study does not establish size extrapolation or transfer to unseen operation types."),
("p","The primary observed target is maximum scheduling delay of a callback requested every 1 ms on the same event loop. Each callback records its lateness relative to its scheduled time and schedules the next callback relative to its actual execution. Monitoring starts before a program and continues through a 4 ms drain afterwards, so that the final computation region is observed. We separately record request duration and each node's elapsed time. The longest region reconstructed from these node timings is diagnostic only and is never supplied as an inference feature."),
("p","The callback target is a sampling-based proxy for uninterrupted execution, not its exact duration. A callback can become due part way through a region; operating-system scheduling and timer resolution also contribute delay. This matters particularly for the shortest programs. Every program is executed five times in a globally shuffled order, and its median maximum delay is used as the evaluation target. Individual repetitions are retained for inspection but are not counted as independent test cases."),
("h2","2.4 Matched programs and evaluation partitions"),
("p","A fixed generator seed, 20261116, creates 160 unique multisets of computation operations. Every family has four variants. Cluster executes all computation before its waits; spread places a true wait between successive computations. Mixed uses the spread order with alternating immediate returns and true waits; cached replaces all those awaits with immediate returns. Cluster and spread have the same operations, sizes, number of waits and suspension flags, and differ only in order. For two-computation families, mixed and cached coincide in effects; they remain members of the same family rather than independent evidence."),
('table','design'),
("p","All variants and repetitions of a family stay in one partition. Table 1 gives the fixed counts. Training and validation contain only two- and three-computation programs. The longer test has 16 families at each of four, six and eight computations. No complete operation multiset is shared across partitions. The generation plan, partition membership, repeat count and candidate comparisons were written before full collection. Pilot timings served only to select practical operation ranges and are excluded from calibration, training and evaluation."),
("h2","2.5 Predictors and information controls"),
("p","Effect neural uses a shared multilayer perceptron to predict log operation duration from four operation indicators and four type-specific log-size features. It has two 16-unit tanh hidden layers, L2 regularisation 0.001 and an L-BFGS optimiser capped at 2,000 iterations. It is fitted to the 48 median calibration values. Exponentiation gives positive node costs, which are composed by (1). Effect power instead fits a separate log-linear size–duration relation for each type and uses the same composition. These predictors do not use request-level training labels."),
("p","Three analytic controls use the power-law node predictions: Sum ignores all suspension boundaries, Largest uses only the most expensive node, and Syntax splits at every await, including cache hits. Keeping their node costs fixed isolates the aggregation rule. Syntax and Effect power differ only in effect interpretation; Effect neural and Effect power differ only in the node regressor. A neural model's advantage over Syntax therefore cannot alone be attributed to learning."),
("p","The unordered ExtraTrees predictor [12] receives, for each operation type, count, sum and maximum size, and sum and maximum calibrated power-law cost. It additionally receives the counts of awaits and genuine suspensions. The ordered ExtraTrees and ordered perceptron receive a padded sequence of up to 15 nodes. Each node has four type indicators, an await indicator, log size, calibrated cost divided by 30 ms and a true-suspension flag. Thus, order-aware controls have the effect information needed to distinguish all variants."),
("p","Both tree ensembles use 400 trees, minimum leaf size two and all available features. The ordered perceptron has 64 and 32 ReLU hidden units, Adam learning rate 0.003, regularisation 0.01 and a 1,500-iteration cap. It learns log callback delay from the 256 training programs. A gated recurrent unit [13] processes the same eight-feature sequence using 32 hidden units. A linear head at each valid position is followed by maximum pooling. It is trained on log delay with Adam, learning rate 0.003 and weight decay 0.0001, for at most 1,000 epochs; validation loss controls an 80-epoch stopping patience. All ten runs reach the epoch cap."),
("p","Learned methods use seeds zero through nine. The reported prediction for each program is the median across their ten predictions; the deterministic analytic controls have one prediction. No test targets select hyperparameters or stopping times. The recurrent model is a deliberately order-aware length-transfer control, not an unrestricted search over recurrent architectures. The request-level models receive both the original descriptors and calibration-derived costs, and also have request labels unavailable to the compositional models."),
("h2","2.6 Transfer checks and HTTP policy replay"),
("p","All experiments run on one Intel Core i7-13620H host under WSL2, Linux 6.6.87.2, Python 3.12.3, NumPy 1.26.0, scikit-learn 1.4.0 and PyTorch 2.13.0. The primary dataset uses asyncio and timer waits: 640 programs times five repetitions gives 3,200 executions. Frozen test programs and predictions are then reused with uvloop 0.22.1, first with timers and then with HTTPX 0.27.0 calls to a separate local HTTP process implementing the same nominal wait. Each transfer condition adds 1,600 test executions without refitting. These are implementation and transport checks on the same hardware."),
("p","Accuracy measures are median absolute percentage error (MdAPE), 90th-percentile absolute percentage error, mean absolute error in milliseconds and the fraction of predictions below half the target. Relative-error denominators are floored at 1 ms. Uncertainty is estimated by 10,000 paired family bootstrap resamples, keeping the four variants together. For six comparisons against Effect neural on the longer primary test, Bonferroni-adjusted percentile intervals use the 0.417th and 99.583rd percentiles. These intervals describe the generated family population and do not account for variation between machines."),
("p","A separate FastAPI 0.111.0 and Uvicorn 0.30.1 server replays the first eight short-test and first eight long-test families, all four variants: 64 programs. An independent client sends lightweight HTTP probes while a heavy program executes. Five policies run in shuffled order with three repeats each, giving 960 executions. The policies execute everything inline, offload everything, or offload when frozen Effect neural, Largest or recurrent predictions exceed a fixed 20 ms budget. Offloading uses a two-process pool; at most one heavy request is active. Predictions are computed before replay, so inference time is excluded from the measured request path."),
("p","For every program and policy, we take the median of the three observed maximum probe round-trip times. Their distribution across the 64 programs is the replay endpoint. This is neither a pooled percentile over all probes nor a throughput benchmark. Heavy-request response time and the offloaded fraction are reported alongside interference. The budget and replay subset were specified before replay, and no policy was tuned against its measurements."),
("h1","3 Results"),
("h2","3.1 Matched operations require execution relations"),
("p","For the 48 longer test families, the median cluster-to-spread ratio of measured blocking is 2.76, while the median ratio of their own request durations is 1.002. Reordering has therefore changed the measured interference much more than completion time in this controlled setting. The median pairwise unavoidable mean absolute error for any common unordered prediction is 13.0 ms. The median pairwise lower bound on worst relative error from (3) is 46.8%. These are bounds within matched pairs, not lower bounds on the MdAPE pooled over all programs."),
("p","At the illustrative 20 ms threshold, 27 of the 48 longer cluster–spread pairs lie on opposite sides. Any policy assigning the same prediction to both must make at least one different decision from the measured-label policy in each such pair. The unordered ensemble indeed produces identical predictions for every matched pair, as checked independently. This result establishes the need to retain relevant order for this task; it does not prefer a graph implementation over an equivalent ordered representation."),
("h2","3.2 Generalisation from short to longer compositions"),
('table','accuracy'),
("p","Table 2 shows a change in which inductive bias is useful. On short unseen families, the recurrent model is most accurate: its MdAPE is 7.0%, compared with 14.2% for Effect neural. On longer compositions, Effect neural reaches 6.7%, compared with 16.9% for the recurrent model. Its long-test mean absolute error is 1.69 ms, against 6.57 ms for the recurrent model. The structurally composed prediction does not need to learn how additional nodes accumulate within a region."),
("p","The paired long-test MdAPE improvement over the recurrent model is 10.2 percentage points; the family-wise adjusted bootstrap interval is reported in the accompanying verification data. The effect is also present across initialisations: individual neural-composition runs have long-test MdAPE from 6.55% to 6.93%, while recurrent runs range from 12.89% to 19.91%. Initialisations are sensitivity checks on a fixed dataset and do not replace the family-based uncertainty analysis."),
('figure',('length.png','Fig. 2 Median relative error by held-out composition length shows the different behaviour of structural and learned aggregation.')),
("p","Fig. 2 separates the three unseen lengths. Ordered trees and the padded perceptron receive correct order but transfer poorly to positions and combinations absent from the short training programs. The recurrent model transfers better than these controls, although it remains less accurate than explicit composition on the longer test. This comparison supports the supplied aggregation rule for the evaluated training regime; it does not show that a sufficiently trained sequence model could never learn it."),
("h2","3.3 Effects matter more than a complex node regressor"),
('table','ablation'),
("p","Table 3 isolates the effect of treating syntax as scheduling behaviour. With all awaits genuinely suspending, Syntax and Effect power use the same partition. With immediate cache returns, Syntax artificially divides computation that actually executes without yielding. Its long-test rate of underprediction by more than a factor of two is 18.2%, compared with zero for both correctly partitioned compositional models. Conversely, Sum avoids this particular underprediction but greatly overestimates separated execution, reaching a long-test 90th-percentile error of 247.8%."),
("p","Effect power is competitive with Effect neural: long-test MdAPE is 7.7% versus 6.7%, and both have approximately 15.6% 90th-percentile error. The neural improvement is small compared with the gains from correct composition, and the multiple-comparison interval does not establish a clear advantage for the neural regressor. The main evidence therefore concerns execution semantics and structural aggregation, rather than a claim that neural calibration is essential."),
('table','transfer'),
("p","Under the two transfer conditions in Table 4, predictions remain frozen. Effect neural has long-test MdAPE of 6.6% with uvloop timers and 5.1% with uvloop HTTP waits. The recurrent model has 15.9% and 16.9%, respectively. Transport changes request completion time and add protocol bookkeeping, but the principal ordering of long-test accuracy is preserved. The observed improvements in some scores under HTTP do not imply that network communication makes prediction intrinsically easier; the callback proxy, timer resolution and measurement variability also change."),
("h2","3.4 Consequences for neighbouring HTTP requests"),
('table','replay'),
("p","In the separate replay summarised in Table 5, the 90th percentile of per-program maximum probe response time falls from 50.3 ms with inline execution to 16.1 ms with the Effect neural rule. The rule offloads 37.5% of programs. Always offloading gives stronger isolation, 2.25 ms at that percentile, but sends every program through the process pool. These policies therefore occupy different operating points rather than proving that partial offloading is universally optimal."),
("p","The median maximum-probe reduction from inline execution is 8.17 ms, with a 95% family-paired bootstrap interval of 1.01–25.43 ms. The recurrent policy offloads 35.9% and achieves 15.7 ms at the replay's 90th percentile, slightly better than Effect neural on this endpoint. Largest offloads 31.25% and achieves 17.1 ms. The numbers of programs whose median maximum probe time exceeds 20 ms are 26 inline, five with Largest, two with the recurrent rule, one with Effect neural and zero when always offloaded."),
("p","Prediction accuracy and replay utility are consequently distinct. The larger accuracy advantage of explicit composition on the full long test does not turn into dominance on every metric of this smaller mixed-length replay. The Effect neural policy also leaves one observed budget violation, so the threshold is not a service-level guarantee. Median heavy-request response time changes from 23.9 ms inline to 25.0 ms under Effect neural and 25.2 ms under universal offloading; the policy protects neighbouring work with a modest observed completion-time cost in this low-concurrency experiment."),
("h1","4 Discussion"),
('p',"The experiment separates learned operation durations from supplied suspension semantics. The recurrent model's short-test advantage shows that both can be learned jointly. Preserving the execution relation explicitly, however, supports length transfer without requiring a request-level learner to extrapolate aggregation. Operation size and environment affect duration, while known suspension annotations determine which node costs accumulate together."),
("p","The graph determines how the predictor combines costs: its annotated edges and nodes specify the uninterrupted regions before the maximum is taken. This is an application of hybrid modelling to a heterogeneous computational domain. The representation has an explicit scope: the results concern paths, and evaluating richer graph formalisms requires an extended experiment. The demonstrated value is the retained relation and its operational meaning, which are represented explicitly rather than inferred from short training examples."),
("p","Several limits matter for deployment. First, true suspension information is given by the configured program. If a cache outcome is unknown before execution, a single fixed annotation is insufficient. One option is to merge uncertain regions conservatively, but this increases offloading; an effect-probability model would require a different validation. Second, the supplied rule models one sequential request. With multiple active heavy requests, ready tasks can execute back to back and produce interference beyond the largest region of either request alone. Queueing, admission interactions and fork–join schedules require a scheduler-level model."),
('p',"Third, four controlled operation families do not represent an external application corpus. Calibration and tests share hardware, libraries and implementations. Cache behaviour, garbage collection, CPU contention and drift can invalidate node costs. The bound (2) exposes this dependence without ensuring that a fitted model satisfies it. Cross-host calibration and validation on unmodified application handlers are needed for broader claims."),
('p',"Fourth, finite callback resolution can favour an end-to-end learner on short programs. Fifth, replay uses one active heavy request and offline predictions, excluding online inference cost, saturation and process-pool queues. Universal offloading provides an isolation reference; it establishes neither throughput nor optimality of the 20 ms threshold."),
("p","Reproducibility materials retain frozen program descriptions, calibration and execution records, node traces, callback observations, partitions, model configurations and predictions. Independent checks reconstruct measured regions and statistics from raw observations. The paired design distinguishes missing information from failure to learn an available relation and can be reused with other operation libraries without assuming that current numerical scores transfer."),
("h1","5 Conclusion"),
('p',"Identical operation multisets can yield different event-loop blocking when suspension positions differ. Correctly annotated paths allow learned operation costs to form a longest-region estimate with a conditional error bound. In this benchmark, composition transfers from two- and three-operation training programs to four-, six- and eight-operation tests more accurately than the evaluated order-aware models. GRU is stronger on short tests, and parametric costs remain competitive. HTTP replay demonstrates practical isolation benefits and residual budget violations within the stated sequential, single-host scope."),
("h1","6 References"),]

